= Introduction <sec:intro>

Locks are among the oldest and most widely deployed synchronization primitives. Embedded key-value stores, database engines, file systems, and the operating system itself rely on them to protect the state that every request must eventually touch @redb @upscaledb. Under steady load a contended lock is never idle: at every hand-off it decides whose code runs next on the shared state. A contended lock is therefore also a _scheduler_ of critical-section time, and like any scheduler it can be fair or unfair.

Patel et al. showed that the classic notion of lock fairness is the wrong one @patel2020scl. A ticket lock or MCS @mellorcrummey1991mcs grants the lock in FIFO order, so each thread acquires it equally often. But when critical sections differ in length, a thread that holds the lock longer on every turn receives more of the machine, and the lock rather than the CPU scheduler decides each thread's share. They called this _scheduler subversion_ and proposed _lock usage fairness_ as the correct goal. The effect is easy to reproduce in a production system. When 1-record and 64-record writers share redb's single-writer lock, MCS achieves a Jain fairness index @jain1984fairness of only 0.85--0.86 over time spent in the critical section. The problem, then, is well understood. This paper is about what it _costs_ to solve it.

Why is it costly? Consider how a lock works. A thread acquires the lock and then runs its own critical section, so the protected data ends up wherever the last holder ran. A lock that wants to be fair must sooner or later hand the lock to a thread on another core, and the data must follow it there. Fairness is therefore paid in _data migration_, and the tighter the fairness, the more often it is paid. Strict FIFO pays it on nearly every hand-off: in redb, MCS incurs 93--126 cross-core cache transfers per transaction and commits 11--19% fewer transactions per second than redb's native mutex. NUMA-aware queue locks such as CNA @dice2019cna and ShflLock @kashyap2019shfllock reorder the waiting queue so that the next holder usually runs on the same socket. This makes each migration cheaper, but the data still moves to a new core on every hand-off; Gupta et al. show that for such locks, including CNA and ShflLock, critical-section time grows with the number of cores @gupta2023tclocks.

Practitioners know this, and a common answer is to relax fairness: a lock need not be fair at every hand-off, only _eventually_. The widely used Rust `parking_lot` crate makes the trade explicit @parkinglot. Its default `Mutex` lets a releasing thread reacquire the lock immediately, avoiding a hand-off to a sleeping waiter, and forces a fair, FIFO unlock only about every 0.5 ms, an idea it takes from WebKit's locks @pizlo2016webkit. Its `FairMutex` hands the lock to the next waiter on every release and is documented as "generally slower, but sometimes needed"; even so, it is fair only in acquisition order. u-SCL's _lock slices_ @patel2020scl follow the same pattern for usage fairness: a slice owner may reacquire the lock freely for a fixed window before ownership passes on. Each of these designs chooses how long one thread may keep the lock before fairness forces a hand-off, and the trade can be made precise. Consider two always-backlogged threads on distinct cores, each critical section costing $c$, under a lock that keeps the gap between their accumulated service within a bound $B$. No thread can then run more than $q_max = floor(2B slash c)$ critical sections in a row, and if each hand-off to the other core migrates state costing at least $M$, the time per operation is at least
$ c + M / q_max. $
An eventually fair lock is one that chooses a loose $B$ in order to amortize $M$. The price is paid in fairness over any window shorter than the bound: redb's native mutex, which has no fairness mechanism at all, is as fast as any lock we measured and also by far the least fair. u-SCL pays differently. Its fairness target, _lock opportunity_,
$ "LOT"(i) = sum "CS"(i) + sum "Lock Idle Time", $
credits idle time as opportunity, so a slice owner that leaves the critical section to run its own code keeps the lock reserved while others wait. u-SCL buys fairness and locality with _work conservation_, and its waiters sleep, which ties its speed to the operating system's wake-up latency and power management. Whether the lock is strictly or eventually fair, the bound says the same thing: fairness and locality are traded against each other.

This trade-off follows from an assumption so basic that it usually goes unstated: the thread that is served is the thread that executes. A lock in fact makes two decisions at every hand-off, _whose_ request is served next and _which core_ runs it, but it makes both with a single choice. Fairness constrains the first decision, and through the coupling it dictates the second. _Delegation_ locks are a special kind of lock that separates them. In flat combining @hendler2010fc, CC-Synch @fatourou2012ccsynch, RCL @lozi2012rcl, ffwd @roghanchi2017ffwd, and, most recently, TCLocks @gupta2023tclocks, threads publish their critical sections as requests, and a single _combiner_ executes all pending requests on their behalf. Execution stays on one core per combining pass, whoever is being served, so a delegation lock need not relax fairness to keep the data local, and the combiner never idles while work is pending. Existing delegation locks have used this separation for speed and, in the case of TCLocks, for transparency: TCLocks ships a waiter's critical-section context to the combiner without any change to application code. Like its predecessors, however, it orders waiters by queue position under policies that target NUMA locality and core oversubscription, not usage, and so remains as usage-unfair as a FIFO lock. In redb, flat combining reaches a usage fairness of only 0.83--0.85. Patel et al. already suggested that delegated requests could be scheduled by usage @patel2020scl; what has been missing is an account of when doing so pays, and a lock that does it.

We observe that the same separation makes fairness cheap. Once execution no longer moves with the lock, the order in which requests are served stops being a question of where the data goes and becomes a pure scheduling decision, made by one thread, at the point where the next critical section is chosen. The combiner already sees every pending request, and because it executes each one itself, it can measure each one's cost for the price of two timestamp reads. Changing _who_ is served next then costs a request hand-off, independent of the size of the protected data.

The separation is amortized, not free, and a simple cost model says when it pays. Let a fair lock in which each thread runs its own critical section spend $h$ per completion on its hand-off protocol and $m$ on moving protected state, and let a usage-ordered delegation lock spend $d$ per request on publication, selection, and accounting, plus $a$ per combining pass that serves $b$ requests. To first order, delegation wins when
$ m + h > d + a / b. $
The model predicts its own failures: delegation should lose when contention is too sparse to form batches ($b$ small), when requests carry large payloads ($d$ large), and when such a lock already keeps the data local ($m$ small). It also predicts that delegation's advantage grows with the size of the working set, since $m$ grows with it and $d$ does not.

We build on this observation with FC-PQ, a _usage-fair delegation_ lock. FC-PQ charges each request's critical-section time to the thread that submitted it and has the combiner serve pending requests in order of lowest accumulated service, in the spirit of virtual-time fair queueing @demers1989wfq. For a fixed, continuously backlogged set of clients, the idealized min-credit rule keeps the spread of accumulated service within one maximal critical section, independent of cost or placement; FC-PQ approximates this rule, and we identify where it departs, notably in how newcomers are credited. A fast path lets an uncontended thread execute directly, so fairness costs nothing when there is no one to be fair to. FC-PQ pursues SCL's goal, a fair share of critical-section time, but in work-conserving form: shares are measured over the time the lock actually spends serving each thread, with no credit for idle time, and whenever any request is pending, the combiner runs one.

We evaluate FC-PQ inside two real databases: redb, where it replaces the single-writer admission so each critical section is a whole write transaction, and UpScaleDB, where it replaces the environment mutex that serializes finds and inserts. We measure fairness over critical-section time charged to the requesting thread, and we fix the CPU clock. Without the latter, u-SCL's sleeping waiters ran at about 1 GHz while spinning locks ran at turbo frequency, which made FC-PQ appear two to three times faster than u-SCL; at a fixed 3.0 GHz that apparent advantage disappears.

The results support the cost argument. In redb, with the clock fixed, FC-PQ reaches a usage fairness of 0.94--0.995, close to u-SCL's 1.00 and well above MCS and flat combining, while running at the speed of the unfair native mutex: with four to eight writers, FC-PQ, flat combining, u-SCL, and the native mutex all commit 29.7--32.3k transactions per second, whereas MCS commits 26.2--26.4k. FC-PQ incurs 10--18 cross-core transfers per transaction against MCS's 93--126. In other words, delegation removes the migration cost that FIFO fairness pays, without the locality bias that the native mutex relies on.

Delegation does not make fairness free, and we report where the cost remains. Redistributing service is itself a cost: because FC-PQ serves short writers more often, it commits 0.72--0.96$times$ the records per second of MCS. Its waiters spin, so it spends about four times the CPU time of u-SCL at equal throughput and assumes no more threads than cores. And on redb, u-SCL matches FC-PQ's throughput, because redb's writers submit back to back, the one case in which reserving the lock never wastes it. We therefore state, as a falsifiable prediction, that FC-PQ's advantage over slice-based locks grows as $min(N, (c + n) / c)$ for $N$ threads with critical-section time $c$ and non-critical time $n$, and that its advantage over fair locks in which threads run their own critical sections grows with the size of the protected working set.

This paper makes the following contributions:

- We argue that the cost of lock usage fairness is structural. Modeling a lock as a service sequence and an executor sequence, we show that when each thread runs its own critical section, a fairness bound limits how long any executor can keep the data, imposing a per-operation migration cost of at least $M slash q_max$.
- We give a cost model for when separating the two sequences through delegation pays, $m + h > d + a slash b$, including the regimes in which it should lose.
- We design FC-PQ, a work-conserving, usage-fair delegation lock that approximates a min-credit rule bounding the service spread among backlogged clients, with a fast path proven sound for the uncontended case.
- We develop a methodology for measuring usage fairness in real systems: service charged to the requester, correctness gates inside the database, and control of the CPU clock. Without clock control, comparisons between spinning and sleeping locks can be dominated by power management rather than lock design.
- We evaluate FC-PQ in redb and UpScaleDB against the native mutex, MCS, flat combining, and u-SCL, and report which costs of fairness delegation removes and which remain.

The rest of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2 reviews scheduler subversion and lock usage fairness. Section 3 develops the two-sequence model, the fairness bound, and the cost model. Section 4 presents the design and implementation of FC-PQ, and Section 5 describes our methodology. Sections 6 and 7 evaluate FC-PQ in redb and UpScaleDB. We discuss limitations in Section 8 and related work in Section 9, and conclude in Section 10.
